      "name": "CalculateBitmaskFromValue",
      "signature": "uint CalculateBitmaskFromValue(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "comment": "Calculates a bitmask value based on an input parameter using conditional logic.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Test if input value (nValue) is zero using TEST instruction\n2. If non-zero, jump to nonzero_case_compute_shift label\n3. If zero, load default return value 0x10 (16) into EAX and return immediately\n4. For non-zero case, add 4 to the input value (LEA ECX, [EAX + 0x4])\n5. Load base value 1 into EAX\n6. Shift left by the computed amount using SHL EAX, CL instruction\n7. Return the calculated bitmask in EAX\n\nParameters:\n  nValue (uint): Input value to calculate bitmask from via EAX register\n    - Valid range: 0 to 31 due to 5-bit shift encoding in x86\n    - Zero is a special case with hardcoded return value 0x10\n\nReturns:\n  EAX (uint): Calculated bitmask value\n    - If nValue == 0: returns 0x10 (16 decimal, serves as default/sentinel)\n    - If nValue != 0: returns (1 << (nValue + 4)) computed via shift\n    - Maximum possible return: depends on shift amount masking\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Zero input returns constant 0x10 instead of computing (1 << 4)\n  - Shift amount constrained to 0-31 due to x86 SHL operation behavior\n  - No bounds checking; shifts wrap around due to CL masking\n  - Function used in Diablo II for calculating display/alignment values",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
      "method": "MNE",
      "index": "MNE:873a43fa8b54c50a1f341eade6a28168",
