      "name": "ReleaseObjectAndCleanup",
      "signature": "void ReleaseObjectAndCleanup(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__d2call",
      "comment": "Release reference-counted object and perform cleanup operations when reference count reaches zero.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Atomically decrement reference count at pRefCountedObject[+0x8] using InterlockedDecrement\n2. Check if reference count equals zero:\n   - If non-zero: Return immediately (object still in use)\n   - If zero: Proceed to cleanup\n3. Load callback function pointer from pServerContext[+0xbc8]\n4. If callback pointer is non-null:\n   a. Load object identifier from pRefCountedObject[+0x0]\n   b. Invoke callback with object identifier, notification code 0x374, and pServerContext\n5. Call RemoveEntryFromHashTables to remove object from all hash tables\n6. Call CleanupPendingSocketOperations to release network resources\n7. Return to caller (callee cleanup via RET 0x8)\n\nParameters:\n- pRefCountedObject (EBX): Pointer to reference-counted object structure\n  - Offset +0x0: Object identifier (DWORD)\n  - Offset +0x8: Reference count (LONG, atomic decrement target)\n- pServerContext (ESI): Pointer to server context/callback management structure\n  - Offset +0xbc8: Optional callback function pointer (may be NULL)\n  - Callback signature: void __cdecl callback(DWORD objectId, DWORD notifyCode, void* pContext)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value\n\nCalling Convention:\n__d2call: First parameter in EBX, second in ESI, callee cleanup (RET 0x8)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Reference count is decremented atomically for thread-safe operation\n- If callback pointer is NULL, cleanup proceeds without invoking callback\n- Constant 0x374 is a notification code passed to callback (semantic: object release event)\n- Both cleanup functions (RemoveEntryFromHashTables, CleanupPendingSocketOperations) must complete regardless of callback result\n- Source file path reference at 0x6ff78fac (szSourceFilePath) suggests this may be called from file I/O operations\n\nStructure Layout Reference:\n- Reference-counted object assumed to be a connection/socket structure\n- Server context is a global/per-thread structure managing connection callbacks and state",
